WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.370 --> 00:00:04.950
It's easy to root for a movie's heroes, but
villains are usually the best part of a movie.

00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:06.620
Maybe it's because they dress cool.

00:00:06.620 --> 00:00:08.630
Maybe it's because they have all the best
lines.

00:00:08.630 --> 00:00:12.570
Or maybe it's because they always look like
they're having way more fun than anybody else

00:00:12.570 --> 00:00:13.570
around them.

00:00:13.570 --> 00:00:18.460
But maybe, just maybe, it's because we occasionally
agree with at least part of the logic behind

00:00:18.460 --> 00:00:20.410
their less than noble endeavors.

00:00:20.410 --> 00:00:21.770
While we'd never, ever condone the dastardly
deeds and malfeasance of these sublimely evil

00:00:21.770 --> 00:00:26.820
characters, we still can't help but think
that they might have had a point.

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:27.820
The Wicked Witch

00:00:27.820 --> 00:00:32.420
Yes, she chases Dorothy halfway around Oz
hell bent on catching her and her adorable

00:00:32.420 --> 00:00:36.530
little dog too, but the Wicked Witch may just
be misunderstood.

00:00:36.530 --> 00:00:40.870
Bloodlust aside, all she really wants is to
get her hands on those glistening ruby slippers

00:00:40.870 --> 00:00:43.219
that Dorothy makes her way across Oz in.

00:00:43.219 --> 00:00:46.859
Don't forget, those magic shoes do not belong
to Dorothy.

00:00:46.859 --> 00:00:50.600
She actually scores them from the lifeless
body of the Wicked Witch's sister -- whom

00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:52.890
she lands on with her house.

00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:55.059
"Slippers, yes."

00:00:55.059 --> 00:00:59.579
From a legal standpoint, Dorothy's possession
of those slippers is questionable at best.

00:00:59.579 --> 00:01:02.979
And one could hardly blame the Wicked Witch
for desperately trying to reclaim a family

00:01:02.979 --> 00:01:06.070
heirloom that rightfully should have passed
to her.

00:01:06.070 --> 00:01:07.210
Roy Batty

00:01:07.210 --> 00:01:12.260
One of the central questions in 1982's Blade
Runner is whether replicants are really people,

00:01:12.260 --> 00:01:14.360
or if they're merely meant for slave labor.

00:01:14.360 --> 00:01:19.000
For big bad Roy Batty, the answer is clear:
He is sentient and he does not want to be

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:22.260
destroyed by Deckard or one of his cohorts
for being some anomaly.

00:01:22.840 --> 00:01:28.240
"I've seen things you people wouldn't believe."

00:01:28.760 --> 00:01:33.100
So, he engages in a massive slaughter spree
that lands him on the Tyrell Corporation's

00:01:33.100 --> 00:01:34.670
most wanted list.

00:01:34.670 --> 00:01:38.480
But Batty doesn't think of himself as some
kink in the system -- he knows he's proof

00:01:38.480 --> 00:01:39.790
of evolution.

00:01:39.790 --> 00:01:44.780
So the question becomes, was his bloody flight
to Earth the act of an evil robot, or was

00:01:44.780 --> 00:01:49.110
it the act of a sentient revolutionary who
just wants to live, and to try and understand

00:01:49.110 --> 00:01:51.530
the humanity with which he's been blessed?

00:01:51.530 --> 00:01:55.590
One should hardly need a Voight-Kampff test
to know the answer to that question.

00:01:55.590 --> 00:01:57.340
Detective Jack Doyle

00:01:57.340 --> 00:02:01.730
There really aren't many good guys to cheer
for in 2007's pitch-black crime drama Gone

00:02:01.730 --> 00:02:02.800
Baby Gone.

00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:07.000
In fact, the film presents a particularly
damning view of metropolitan Boston, brimming

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:09.840
with drunks, druggies, gang bangers, and more.

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:13.599
But the biggest baddie is none other than
Morgan Freeman's unassuming Detective Jack

00:02:13.599 --> 00:02:17.970
Doyle, who's guilty of kidnapping of a 4-year-old
girl and colluding with his police buddies

00:02:17.970 --> 00:02:19.549
to cover up the crime.

00:02:19.549 --> 00:02:22.909
But the crime may not be as immoral as it
seems on the surface.

00:02:22.909 --> 00:02:25.280
"Don't come in here trying to get noble, boy."

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:29.090
See, the parents of the missing girl turn
out to be a no good junkie of a father and

00:02:29.090 --> 00:02:33.360
a raging drunk of a mother with absolutely
no parental instincts or moral compass.

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:37.769
So, they aren't exactly upstanding citizens,
and Doyle's abduction is really just a noble

00:02:37.769 --> 00:02:41.159
attempt to give the whip-smart little girl
a real shot at life.

00:02:41.159 --> 00:02:45.540
And nobody—not even Casey Affleck's do-gooding
private detective—believes she's going to

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:47.379
get that with her mother.

00:02:47.379 --> 00:02:49.040
Principal Ed Rooney

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:51.719
It's almost impossible to like Principal Rooney.

00:02:51.719 --> 00:02:56.180
He's stuck up, he's rude, he's got that awful
mustache, and he has it out for that most

00:02:56.180 --> 00:02:58.319
lovable of scamps, Ferris Bueller.

00:02:58.319 --> 00:03:02.090
But if you stop and think about Ferris Bueller's
Day Off for even half a second, you might

00:03:02.090 --> 00:03:04.670
start to see that Bueller is no prize himself.

00:03:04.670 --> 00:03:09.319
He's brash, cocky and overbearing—and he's
wearing a sweater vest for crying out loud.

00:03:09.319 --> 00:03:11.049
That alone is a crime of fashion.

00:03:11.049 --> 00:03:14.629
So, suffice it to say, he needs some kinda
lesson to get his act together.

00:03:14.629 --> 00:03:19.180
Who better to teach that lesson than a dedicated
educator and impassioned public servant like

00:03:19.180 --> 00:03:20.220
Ed Rooney?

00:03:20.220 --> 00:03:24.329
We can all agree that Principal Rooney took
matters a tad too personally—and ultimately

00:03:24.329 --> 00:03:27.730
way too far—but we can't help but admit
that he was kinda right.

00:03:27.730 --> 00:03:31.909
Ferris Bueller is a total wisenheimer who
lies to his parents, skips school, bullies

00:03:31.909 --> 00:03:37.540
his best friend, steals a priceless car, and
impersonates a distinguished sausage entrepreneur

00:03:37.540 --> 00:03:41.719
for the purpose of stealing his reservation
at a fine-dining establishment.

00:03:41.719 --> 00:03:44.430
And he gets away with all of it.

00:03:44.430 --> 00:03:45.859
Miranda Priestly

00:03:45.859 --> 00:03:50.439
The Devil Wears Prada's fashion-savvy villainess
is condescending, self-centered, and not afraid

00:03:50.439 --> 00:03:55.180
to demand perfection from everyone around
her—especially her new assistant, Andy.

00:03:55.180 --> 00:03:58.739
But then, she is the head honcho of a massive
publishing empire.

00:03:58.739 --> 00:04:03.290
And any woman running a billion dollar company
in a male-dominated business environment is

00:04:03.290 --> 00:04:06.840
probably being held to a different standard
than her male counterparts.

00:04:06.840 --> 00:04:10.579
Which means nothing less than perfection will
suffice to keep her on top.

00:04:10.579 --> 00:04:11.639
"Which exposed the…"

00:04:11.639 --> 00:04:12.639
"That's all."

00:04:12.639 --> 00:04:15.239
So how can she expect anything less from the
people around her?

00:04:15.239 --> 00:04:19.500
As hard as Miranda is on Andy, she's just
as hard on everyone else.

00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:23.040
It's worth noting, too, that Miranda's approach
actually works.

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:26.930
Throughout the film she manages to pull the
best out of every single person around her,

00:04:26.930 --> 00:04:28.500
including Andy.

00:04:28.500 --> 00:04:29.920
The U.S. government

00:04:29.920 --> 00:04:33.569
Let's face it, pegging the U.S. government
as a villain is not as hard is it used to

00:04:33.569 --> 00:04:34.569
be.

00:04:34.569 --> 00:04:39.310
But back in 1982, E.T.: The Extra Terrestrial
had to exaggerate their threat factor a bit

00:04:39.310 --> 00:04:43.210
by making them gun-wielding goons who strongarm
kids just trying to have a good time with

00:04:43.210 --> 00:04:45.040
their new otherworldly pal.

00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:48.060
But their bad rap might be misplaced in this
movie.

00:04:48.060 --> 00:04:52.290
After all, they are investigating what may
or may not be a hostile alien invasion -- and

00:04:52.290 --> 00:04:55.900
without access to the creature, how are they
supposed to know he's a lover, not a fighter?

00:04:59.740 --> 00:05:01.080
"Ouuuuuuch."

00:05:01.260 --> 00:05:05.140
They could just ask Elliott, of course, but
as far as they know the powerful psychic bond

00:05:05.150 --> 00:05:09.620
the boy shares with the alien being is just
some unknown form of mind control.

00:05:09.620 --> 00:05:14.030
In the end, those agents are a bit coarser
than they have to be, but if and when a real

00:05:14.030 --> 00:05:18.360
alien invasion happens, you'd probably want
the feds to take all precautions, wouldn't

00:05:18.360 --> 00:05:19.360
you?

00:05:19.360 --> 00:05:21.340
General Francis Hummel

00:05:21.340 --> 00:05:25.590
Speaking of federal foes, The Rock's General
Hummel is a guy responsible for instigating

00:05:25.590 --> 00:05:30.680
a hostage crisis on Alcatraz and threatening
to unleash weaponized nerve gas on San Francisco

00:05:30.680 --> 00:05:32.940
if the government doesn't pay him $100 million
bucks.

00:05:32.940 --> 00:05:37.669
But really, Hummel has no intention of releasing
any chemical weapons on anyone.

00:05:37.669 --> 00:05:41.330
He only leads the abhorrent mission in an
effort to get compensation for families of

00:05:41.330 --> 00:05:45.120
fallen soldiers after the government refuses
to honor or support them.

00:05:45.120 --> 00:05:47.810
He even tries to do it the honest way first.

00:05:47.810 --> 00:05:51.240
Did we mention that the "ransom" money was
to be collected from an illegal government

00:05:51.240 --> 00:05:52.620
slush-fund too?

00:05:52.620 --> 00:05:56.790
Call us crazy, but Hummel sounds more like
an impassioned advocate for veterans' rights

00:05:56.790 --> 00:05:58.240
than a villain.

00:05:58.240 --> 00:05:59.590
Walter Peck

00:05:59.590 --> 00:06:04.199
Ghostbusters baddie Walter Peck is exactly
the sort of snooty, stick-up-his-butt bureaucrat

00:06:04.199 --> 00:06:08.830
you'd expect to show up at your door if you've
got an unlicensed, unregulated and massively

00:06:08.830 --> 00:06:13.730
unstable nuclear reactor tucked away in the
basement of a near-condemned building.

00:06:13.730 --> 00:06:17.860
Even if Peck is boorish and confrontational
and easy to hate, he's still just doing his

00:06:17.860 --> 00:06:20.530
job of protecting the environment from harm.

00:06:20.530 --> 00:06:23.090
"Yes, it's true.

00:06:23.090 --> 00:06:24.539
This man has no d---."

00:06:24.539 --> 00:06:29.080
Yes, everything is holding okay until Peck
turns off the electricity, but how is he supposed

00:06:29.080 --> 00:06:33.460
to know he'd be opening the gates to a near
apocalyptic supernatural event?

00:06:33.460 --> 00:06:34.659
Syndrome

00:06:34.659 --> 00:06:39.280
It's an age-old question as to whether villains
are the result of nature or nurture, but in

00:06:39.280 --> 00:06:42.879
the case of The Incredibles, the latter certainly
seems to be true.

00:06:42.879 --> 00:06:46.960
After all, Buddy Pine is a born genius who
was a wizard with tech and capable of greatness

00:06:46.960 --> 00:06:48.199
even as a young man.

00:06:48.199 --> 00:06:51.199
And he really, really, really wants to be
a hero.

00:06:51.199 --> 00:06:52.840
"Who are you supposed to be?"

00:06:52.840 --> 00:06:54.020
"Well, I'm Incrediboy."

00:06:54.020 --> 00:06:58.349
When he tries to join up with Frozone and
the Incredible family, however, those "heroes"

00:06:58.349 --> 00:07:02.639
respond with excessive scorn and ridicule
before ostracizing the gifted young inventor

00:07:02.639 --> 00:07:05.530
for not being blessed with superhuman abilities.

00:07:05.530 --> 00:07:09.759
It's their cruel shunning that gives birth
to the genius super-villain Syndrome, so one

00:07:09.759 --> 00:07:14.189
could hardly blame him for harboring a deep
rooted resentment towards the conceited superheroes

00:07:14.189 --> 00:07:15.510
who wouldn't let him play along.

00:07:15.510 --> 00:07:17.860
"This isn't the end of it!"

00:07:17.860 --> 00:07:19.719
The Xenomorph Queen

00:07:19.719 --> 00:07:24.350
Picture this: you're living a quiet life on
a desolate planet so far out in deep space

00:07:24.350 --> 00:07:27.849
that virtually no other living being can even
find you.

00:07:27.849 --> 00:07:32.979
Then one day, spaceships show up full of alien
creatures sent by a greedy corporation to

00:07:32.979 --> 00:07:38.470
rob your planet of precious minerals and—let's
be honest—probably destroy your home's ecosystem

00:07:38.470 --> 00:07:39.479
in the process.

00:07:39.479 --> 00:07:43.629
That, in a nutshell, is what the Alien story
might look like through the eyes of the Xenomorph

00:07:43.629 --> 00:07:44.629
Queen.

00:07:44.629 --> 00:07:47.289
It's kinda like Avatar, but without the pretty
blue people.

00:07:47.289 --> 00:07:51.889
The Xenomorph Queen might serve as the central
villain in the Alien franchise, but for her,

00:07:51.889 --> 00:07:56.420
these human invaders are pretty hell bent
on capturing, weaponizing, or outright destroying

00:07:56.420 --> 00:07:58.580
her legions of unborn children.

00:07:58.580 --> 00:08:02.819
So the question becomes, is the Xenomorph
Queen an evil, alien creature out to destroy

00:08:02.819 --> 00:08:03.819
humans?

00:08:03.819 --> 00:08:09.080
Or is she a doting mother out to protect her
home, her family, and maybe her entire species?

00:08:09.080 --> 00:08:10.449
Elijah Price

00:08:10.449 --> 00:08:13.969
Unbreakable's Elijah Price is definitely not
one of the good guys.

00:08:13.969 --> 00:08:17.740
With countless casualties caused by his hand
and the full breadth of his treachery still

00:08:17.740 --> 00:08:22.139
unknown, Elijah is basically the poster boy
for homegrown terrorism.

00:08:22.139 --> 00:08:26.629
And no, his own tragic circumstances don't
even come close to excusing his actions.

00:08:26.629 --> 00:08:30.879
"In a comic, you know how you can tell who
the archvillain's going to be?

00:08:30.879 --> 00:08:33.950
He's the exact opposite of the hero."

00:08:33.950 --> 00:08:38.700
But at the end of the day, those deplorable
acts do exactly what Elijah expects them to

00:08:38.700 --> 00:08:43.779
do—find someone on the opposite end of the
spectrum as himself: someone whose bones didn't

00:08:43.779 --> 00:08:45.389
shatter like glass.

00:08:45.389 --> 00:08:48.310
Someone strong as a team of oxen and tough
as a tank.

00:08:48.310 --> 00:08:51.180
Someone who could only be classified as a
superhero.

00:08:51.180 --> 00:08:53.550
That's exactly what David Dunne turns out
to be.

00:08:53.550 --> 00:08:57.940
And try to remember that Dunne may never embrace
his gifts if Elijah Price doesn't push him

00:08:57.940 --> 00:08:58.940
to do so.

00:08:58.940 --> 00:09:03.370
That might been enough for Price to claim
hero status himself—if not for all the pyromania

00:09:03.370 --> 00:09:05.670
and bombings and such.

00:09:05.670 --> 00:09:06.670
Jigsaw

00:09:06.670 --> 00:09:09.810
We're not going to say we saved the "best"
for last, because there isn't much to like

00:09:09.810 --> 00:09:13.360
when it comes to the torturous psychopath
we've come to know as Jigsaw.

00:09:13.360 --> 00:09:18.420
As far as movie villains go, John "Jigsaw"
Kramer is pretty much the worst of the worst.

00:09:18.420 --> 00:09:22.230
But it's still important to note that, while
he doesn't make it easy for some people to

00:09:22.230 --> 00:09:26.600
live, he technically never ends anyone's life
with his own hands.

00:09:26.600 --> 00:09:30.690
His twisted traps and devices are puzzles
meant to be solved so that the people trapped

00:09:30.690 --> 00:09:35.180
inside—most of whom have questionable morals
to begin with—can escape with a newfound

00:09:35.180 --> 00:09:37.490
respect for their miserable little lives.

00:09:37.490 --> 00:09:42.050
It's also worth noting that those who do escape
ultimately do seem to find new purpose.

00:09:42.050 --> 00:09:44.120
"And brought meaning to it."

00:09:44.120 --> 00:09:49.140
Throw in Jigsaw's own backstory—as a dad-to-be
who tragically loses everything before surviving

00:09:49.140 --> 00:09:53.620
his own near-death experience—and it's a
little easier to see him as a radical life

00:09:53.620 --> 00:09:54.740
counselor.

00:09:54.740 --> 00:09:59.220
Of course, to do that you'd have to overlook
all of the torture and blood on his evil hands.

00:09:59.220 --> 00:10:01.600
Hey, nobody's perfect, right?

00:10:01.600 --> 00:10:02.600
Thanks for watching!

00:10:02.600 --> 00:10:05.100
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:10:05.100 --> 00:10:07.160
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

